WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.162
You must find the smallest buffer size that never drops a frame. Testing every size is slow. Binary searching the answer finds it in a few tries.

00:00:12.362 --> 00:00:13.291
Pause and think.

00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:16.629
What property must hold to binary search an answer?

00:00:17.029 --> 00:00:24.343
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:31.224
This lesson covers binary search, including the powerful trick of binary searching the answer itself.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:40.143
New word: binary search. Find an item in sorted data by halving the range each step. Picture it like this: Guessing a number with "higher or lower".

00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:47.511
Think of guessing a number between one and a hundred, where a friend says higher or lower.

00:00:47.961 --> 00:00:53.139
A smart guesser always picks the middle. Each answer throws away half of the remaining numbers.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:57.676
After only seven guesses you have found any number between one and a hundred.

00:00:58.126 --> 00:01:01.133
That halving is why binary search costs log n.

00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:05.791
Here is a sorted array, and we search for the value nine.

00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:14.283
New word: array. Items stored side by side in memory, reached by index. Picture it like this: A row of numbered lockers.

00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:23.115
Low is zero, high is seven, so mid is three, holding seven. Seven is smaller than nine, so search the right half.

00:01:23.565 --> 00:01:28.151
Now mid is five, holding eleven. Too big, so search the left part.

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:31.794
Mid is four, holding nine. Found in three steps.

00:01:33.144 --> 00:01:36.580
Now the more powerful idea: searching for an answer.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:42.464
In Koko Eating Bananas, we want the slowest eating speed that still finishes in time.

00:01:42.914 --> 00:01:50.623
If a speed works, every faster speed also works. The answers look like no, no, no, yes, yes, yes.

00:01:51.073 --> 00:01:56.018
Binary search finds the first yes. Each check costs one pass over the piles.

00:01:57.368 --> 00:01:58.924
A little vocabulary.

00:01:59.374 --> 00:02:05.017
The invariant is the promise your loop keeps, for example: the answer is always between low and high.

00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:12.142
Lower bound finds the first element greater than or equal to x. Upper bound finds the first element greater than x.

00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:19.059
A monotonic predicate is a yes or no test that flips only once. If you can find one, you can binary search.

00:02:20.409 --> 00:02:21.605
The classic bugs.

00:02:22.055 --> 00:02:27.314
Overflow in low plus high, so write low plus high minus low divided by two.

00:02:27.764 --> 00:02:32.849
Infinite loops when low equals mid; pick one loop style and stick to it.

00:02:33.299 --> 00:02:38.257
And off by one at the edges. Test with arrays of size zero, one and two.

00:02:38.359 --> 00:02:48.738
New word: edge. A connection between two nodes in a graph; also "the edge" = servers close to users. Picture it like this: A road between two cities.

00:02:51.288 --> 00:02:58.219
If the data is sorted, or the answer is monotonic, think binary search. Try Koko, then Median of Two Sorted Arrays.

00:02:58.207 --> 00:02:59.461
Back to the question.

00:02:59.861 --> 00:03:02.798
What property must hold to binary search an answer?

00:03:03.198 --> 00:03:07.343
Monotonic: once an answer works, every bigger one works too.

00:03:08.543 --> 00:03:09.414
Quick recap.

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:11.195
Halve the range each step.

00:03:11.595 --> 00:03:13.534
Watch the loop bounds carefully.

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:16.187
You can binary search the answer itself.
